Viral Gastroenteritis (Norovirus)
Hydration, Fluid and Volume abnormalities Gastrointestinal Loss
Explosive severe vomiting, Copious non-bloody watery diarrhea, Rapid spread in confined environment

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HFV-049
Reason for Visit: Sudden, explosive onset of severe vomiting and massive watery diarrhea in an elderly patient, requiring urgent assessment for severe acute dehydration and shock.
Location: Residential Aged Care Facility
Present at Visit: Patient and facility nursing staff
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a resident who suddenly developed continuous, violent vomiting and uncontrollable diarrhea, resulting in severe lethargy and an inability to maintain oral intake. This hyperacute fluid loss mandated an urgent on-site evaluation to assess intravascular volume, rule out an acute surgical abdomen, and provide parenteral antiemetics to prevent progression to hypovolemic shock prior to standard morning hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Unstoppable vomiting, massive watery diarrhea, and profound weakness. Nursing staff state, It started violently three hours ago; she is vomiting everything up simultaneously with explosive diarrhea, and she looks completely grey and exhausted.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms commenced with an acute, explosive onset of severe, non-bilious vomiting just three hours ago. This was immediately followed by concurrent copious, non-bloody watery diarrhea. Staff note a rapid spread within the confined environment of the aged care facility, with three other residents developing identical symptoms today. The patient complains of severe, diffuse abdominal cramping immediately preceding episodes of emesis or diarrhea. There is a low-grade fever. Profound systemic myalgias and a severe headache are reported. The staff note decreased urine output (oliguria) and intensely dark urine over the last 6 hours. The patient exhibits severe generalized lethargy.
Functional Status: Bedbound and actively vomiting; fully reliant on nursing staff for all care and hygiene.
Social/Safety Concerns: Immediate risk of profound hypovolemic shock and severe outbreak transmission; strict barrier nursing implemented.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 95/60 mmHg | HR: 115 bpm | Temp: 37.6 C | RR: 20 bpm | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 60.0 Kg
General: Patient appears acutely unwell, pale, diaphoretic, and is holding an emesis basin.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Tachycardia is marked, indicating mild to moderate intravascular volume depletion. Weak radial pulses. Postural blood pressure check deferred due to active emesis and profound lethargy. Respiratory: Lungs are clear. Gastrointestinal: The diagnostic triad is clinically classic. There is witnessed explosive severe vomiting and copious non-bloody watery diarrhea. The history confirms rapid spread in a confined environment. The abdomen is diffusely tender with hyperactive bowel sounds, but soft and entirely lacking rebound, guarding, or focal rigidity to suggest a surgical emergency. Integumentary: Examination reveals dry oral mucous membranes, a coated tongue, and sluggish skin turgor.
Environmental Observations: Patient is isolated in a single room with full contact and droplet precautions in place. Emesis basin contains clear gastric fluid.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against facility chart. No recent initiation of new medications or antibiotics.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Viral Gastroenteritis (Norovirus) - Status: Acute, decompensating volume status
Differentials:

Bacterial Enteritis (e.g., Salmonella, Campylobacter) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The explosive predominance of vomiting combined with the extremely rapid, clustered outbreak pattern characteristic of Norovirus, lacking the high spiking fevers and gross bloody stools typical of invasive bacteria.

Bowel Obstruction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of massive, concurrent watery diarrhea, completely negating absolute constipation, alongside a soft, non-distended abdomen.

Clostridioides difficile Infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of recent broad-spectrum antibiotic exposure, the prominence of vomiting, and the highly contagious outbreak cluster affecting non-antibiotic treated residents.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits the classic, hyperacute presentation of Norovirus gastroenteritis. The explosive, simultaneous vomiting and copious watery diarrhea have caused extremely rapid and significant sensible fluid loss, resulting in moderate hypovolemia evidenced by tachycardia, hypotension (BP 95/60), and oliguria. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period in this frail demographic would have inevitably resulted in severe hypovolemic shock and potential acute kidney injury. Urgent after-hours assessment was critical to definitively rule out a surgical abdomen, confirm the benign but severe viral etiology, administer immediate parenteral antiemetic therapy to halt the massive upper GI losses, and initiate a highly controlled, micro-dosing oral rehydration protocol under nursing supervision. The expected investigation results include detection of Norovirus RNA in stool samples via multiplex polymerase chain reaction (PCR) assay that would assist in confirming the diagnosis for facility outbreak management protocols.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Ondansetron 4mg sublingually immediately. Prescribed a second dose of Ondansetron 4mg wafer PRN in 8 hours. Directed nursing staff to commence strict oral rehydration therapy utilizing an ice-cold electrolyte solution only after 30 minutes of no emesis.
Positive Behavioral Change: (Directed to nursing staff): To safely reverse her dehydration without triggering a violent vomiting reflex, commit to administering exactly one 5ml syringe of ice-cold electrolyte fluid every 10 minutes. Use a timer. When the timer goes off, administer the fluid, and explicitly record it on the fluid balance chart. The visual record of retained fluid and the patient's improving pulse rate will reinforce your strict adherence to this slow, laborious micro-dosing process.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: GP clinical review tomorrow morning to reassess perfusion and volume status.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Proceed to the Emergency Department immediately or call 000 if the patient becomes completely unarousable, if the blood pressure drops below 85 systolic despite oral fluids, or if they develop severe, rigid, and constant abdominal pain.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This is a highly contagious, acute viral illness requiring strict isolation and meticulous fluid management in the elderly.
Clinical Course: The virus causes the stomach and bowel to violently empty themselves. This intense phase is typically self-limiting and burns out rapidly within 24 to 48 hours. The absolute priority is slowly replacing the massive amount of fluid lost to keep the blood pressure safe until the virus passes.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to rapidly push large cups of water or tea. Rapidly filling the irritated stomach will immediately trigger further violent vomiting, dangerously worsening the dehydration.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The violent vomiting is rapidly dropping the blood pressure. The wafer under the tongue turns off the vomiting center in the brain, giving us a small window to slowly trickle specialized salt water back into the system to stabilize the heart rate.
Actionable Steps: Nursing staff to isolate the patient. Administer 5ml of electrolyte fluid every 10 minutes. Maintain strict fluid balance charts.

